1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,937
This is what the night sky should look like.

2
00:00:02,937 --> 00:00:03,559
Not black.

3
00:00:03,559 --> 00:00:09,095
A wall of light, as bright as the surface of the Sun, in every direction, all night long.

4
00:00:09,095 --> 00:00:15,378
Our galaxy alone holds hundreds of billions of stars, and there are hundreds of billions of galaxies.

5
00:00:15,378 --> 00:00:16,498
So why is it dark?

6
00:00:17,198 --> 00:00:26,281
That question bothered astronomers for four hundred years, and the answer turned out to be one of the strongest clues we have about how everything began.

7
00:00:26,281 --> 00:00:28,240
Today we treat it as a case file.

8
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:29,071
Four suspects.

9
00:00:29,071 --> 00:00:33,049
One by one, we clear them, until we find who turned out the lights.

10
00:00:33,749 --> 00:00:36,373
Why Is Space Black If There Are Billions of Stars?

11
00:00:37,073 --> 00:00:39,082
First, why this is a real puzzle.

12
00:00:39,082 --> 00:00:42,005
Imagine standing in the middle of a huge forest.

13
00:00:42,005 --> 00:00:44,746
Close by, you can see gaps between the trees.

14
00:00:44,746 --> 00:00:49,922
But if the forest goes on far enough, every line of sight eventually ends on a trunk.

15
00:00:49,922 --> 00:00:52,175
Look any direction, and you see wood.

16
00:00:52,175 --> 00:00:53,941
Now swap the trees for stars.

17
00:00:53,941 --> 00:00:59,665
In an endless universe full of stars, every line of sight should end on the surface of a star.

18
00:01:00,365 --> 00:01:07,133
The puzzle carries the name of Heinrich Olbers, a German astronomer who wrote about it in eighteen twenty three.

19
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:13,479
But Johannes Kepler was already using it in sixteen ten, to argue that the stars could not go on forever.

20
00:01:13,479 --> 00:01:15,171
Others took it on after him.

21
00:01:15,171 --> 00:01:17,044
A poet, and a famous physicist.

22
00:01:17,044 --> 00:01:19,885
We'll meet them both before the case is closed.

23
00:01:20,585 --> 00:01:22,306
How bright would that sky be?

24
00:01:22,306 --> 00:01:27,053
The Sun covers a tiny patch of sky, smaller than your fingertip at arm's length.

25
00:01:27,053 --> 00:01:34,827
By our calculation, it would take about ninety two thousand Suns, packed edge to edge, to tile the half of the sky above your head.

26
00:01:34,827 --> 00:01:40,583
Day and night, the sky would glow like the surface of a star, at more than five thousand degrees.

27
00:01:40,583 --> 00:01:42,601
Nothing on Earth could survive it.

28
00:01:43,301 --> 00:01:46,639
One quick thing first, so we're asking the right question.

29
00:01:46,639 --> 00:01:51,416
Astronauts on the Moon saw a black sky even at noon, with the Sun blazing overhead.

30
00:01:51,416 --> 00:01:56,309
Our sky is blue in the daytime only because air scatters sunlight in every direction.

31
00:01:56,309 --> 00:01:59,129
Take the air away and the daytime sky goes black.

32
00:01:59,129 --> 00:02:00,971
But that doesn't solve anything.

33
00:02:00,971 --> 00:02:04,597
Air doesn't explain why the darkness between the stars is dark.

34
00:02:04,597 --> 00:02:08,453
Out there, there's no air at all, and the paradox is still waiting.

35
00:02:09,153 --> 00:02:10,375
Suspect number one.

36
00:02:10,375 --> 00:02:13,269
Faraway stars are simply too faint to matter.

37
00:02:13,269 --> 00:02:14,620
It sounds convincing.

38
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:18,607
Move twice as far from a star, and it looks four times dimmer.

39
00:02:18,607 --> 00:02:22,209
Move ten times farther, and it's a hundred times dimmer.

40
00:02:22,909 --> 00:02:27,478
But picture the stars arranged in shells around you, like layers of an onion.

41
00:02:27,478 --> 00:02:32,700
A shell twice as far away has four times the area, so it holds four times as many stars.

42
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:36,498
Each one is four times dimmer, but there are four times as many.

43
00:02:36,498 --> 00:02:39,287
Every shell sends you the same amount of light.

44
00:02:39,287 --> 00:02:45,043
Stack up endless shells, and the light keeps adding up, until nearer stars block the ones behind.

45
00:02:45,043 --> 00:02:47,357
Distance alone can't make the sky dark.

46
00:02:48,057 --> 00:02:49,447
Suspect one, cleared.

47
00:02:49,447 --> 00:02:50,704
Suspect number two.

48
00:02:50,704 --> 00:02:51,035
Dust.

49
00:02:51,035 --> 00:02:55,867
Space really is full of gas and dust, and it really does block starlight.

50
00:02:55,867 --> 00:02:59,971
It's why the center of our own galaxy is hidden from our eyes.

51
00:02:59,971 --> 00:03:02,883
Olbers himself believed this was the answer.

52
00:03:03,584 --> 00:03:04,418
But it fails.

53
00:03:04,418 --> 00:03:07,373
Dust that soaks up light also soaks up energy.

54
00:03:07,373 --> 00:03:17,584
In an ageless universe drowning in starlight, the dust would keep warming until it was as hot as the stars themselves, and then it would glow just as brightly.

55
00:03:17,584 --> 00:03:20,024
A curtain can hide a fire for a while.

56
00:03:20,024 --> 00:03:20,795
Not forever.

57
00:03:20,795 --> 00:03:22,143
Suspect two, cleared.

58
00:03:22,843 --> 00:03:24,111
Suspect number three.

59
00:03:24,111 --> 00:03:25,801
The universe has a birthday.

60
00:03:25,801 --> 00:03:27,975
Light is fast, but it isn't instant.

61
00:03:27,975 --> 00:03:32,985
The light from the Andromeda Galaxy left it about two and a half million years ago.

62
00:03:32,985 --> 00:03:36,305
The farther you look, the further back in time you see.

63
00:03:37,005 --> 00:03:40,828
And the universe is about thirteen point eight billion years old.

64
00:03:40,828 --> 00:03:41,946
So there's a limit.

65
00:03:41,946 --> 00:03:46,063
Past a certain distance, light simply hasn't had time to get here yet.

66
00:03:46,063 --> 00:03:48,651
We live inside a bubble of visible universe.

67
00:03:48,651 --> 00:03:53,474
Beyond its edge there may be countless stars, but their light is still on the way.

68
00:03:54,174 --> 00:03:56,823
One detail makes the bubble even stranger.

69
00:03:56,823 --> 00:04:06,094
Because space kept stretching while that light was traveling, the most distant objects we can see are now about forty six billion light years away.

70
00:04:06,094 --> 00:04:08,932
That's the radius of the observable universe.

71
00:04:08,932 --> 00:04:09,248
Huge.

72
00:04:09,248 --> 00:04:12,969
And still, by the standards of this paradox, far too small.

73
00:04:13,669 --> 00:04:15,166
Now go back to the forest.

74
00:04:15,166 --> 00:04:19,026
How deep would it have to be for every line of sight to hit a star?

75
00:04:19,026 --> 00:04:23,461
By our rough calculation, deeper than a hundred billion trillion light years.

76
00:04:23,461 --> 00:04:27,954
That's trillions of times farther than light has traveled since the beginning.

77
00:04:27,954 --> 00:04:29,048
The forest is real.

78
00:04:29,048 --> 00:04:32,101
We can only see a small clearing in the middle of it.

79
00:04:32,801 --> 00:04:34,666
And stars don't shine forever.

80
00:04:34,666 --> 00:04:37,774
A star like the Sun lasts about ten billion years.

81
00:04:37,774 --> 00:04:43,059
To fill the sky with light, stars would have to keep shining for unimaginably longer.

82
00:04:43,059 --> 00:04:45,980
Lord Kelvin worked this out in nineteen oh one.

83
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:48,840
But the first to guess it was not a scientist.

84
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:52,384
It was the poet Edgar Allan Poe, in eighteen forty eight.

85
00:04:52,384 --> 00:04:53,876
The dark sky is a clock.

86
00:04:53,876 --> 00:04:55,244
Suspect three, guilty.

87
00:04:55,944 --> 00:04:58,831
Poe's words, from his essay Eureka, still hold up.

88
00:04:58,831 --> 00:05:11,362
He wrote that the only way to understand the empty gaps our telescopes find in countless directions would be to suppose the invisible background is so far away that no ray from it has yet been able to reach us at all.

89
00:05:11,362 --> 00:05:18,061
A poet, working from pure reasoning, more than eighty years before astronomers learned the universe had a beginning.

90
00:05:18,061 --> 00:05:19,621
He didn't have the numbers.

91
00:05:19,621 --> 00:05:20,776
But he had the idea.

92
00:05:21,475 --> 00:05:23,426
But this case has an accomplice.

93
00:05:23,426 --> 00:05:24,644
Suspect number four.

94
00:05:24,644 --> 00:05:26,290
Space itself is stretching.

95
00:05:26,290 --> 00:05:36,345
Since Edwin Hubble's work in the nineteen twenties, we've known that distant galaxies are moving away from us, and the farther away they are, the faster they recede.

96
00:05:37,045 --> 00:05:40,699
As light crosses expanding space, its waves get stretched.

97
00:05:40,699 --> 00:05:42,148
Blue shifts toward red.

98
00:05:42,148 --> 00:05:43,723
Red shifts into infrared.

99
00:05:43,723 --> 00:05:49,204
The most distant galaxies have been stretched right out of the colors our eyes can see.

100
00:05:49,204 --> 00:05:53,677
That's why the James Webb Space Telescope was built to see in infrared.

101
00:05:53,677 --> 00:05:55,315
That old light isn't gone.

102
00:05:55,315 --> 00:05:57,205
It's been dragged out of view.

103
00:05:57,905 --> 00:06:00,141
Stretched light also loses energy.

104
00:06:00,141 --> 00:06:08,625
The James Webb Space Telescope has caught a galaxy whose light left it only about three hundred million years after the Big Bang.

105
00:06:08,625 --> 00:06:10,861
That light set out as ultraviolet.

106
00:06:10,861 --> 00:06:15,662
Its waves are now about fifteen times longer, and it arrives as infrared.

107
00:06:15,662 --> 00:06:18,555
To our eyes, that galaxy is perfectly black.

108
00:06:19,255 --> 00:06:20,371
And now the twist.

109
00:06:20,371 --> 00:06:22,230
The sky is not actually black.

110
00:06:22,230 --> 00:06:30,781
About three hundred and eighty thousand years after the Big Bang, the entire universe was a glowing fog, at around three thousand degrees.

111
00:06:30,781 --> 00:06:33,012
In every direction, a wall of light.

112
00:06:33,012 --> 00:06:35,738
Exactly the sky that Olbers was looking for.

113
00:06:35,738 --> 00:06:39,394
And that wall is still out there, surrounding us right now.

114
00:06:40,094 --> 00:06:43,829
But since then, space has stretched about eleven hundred times.

115
00:06:43,829 --> 00:06:51,832
That glow has cooled to just two point seven degrees above absolute zero, and its light has been stretched all the way into microwaves.

116
00:06:51,832 --> 00:06:57,582
It's called the cosmic microwave background, and it was found by accident in nineteen sixty five.

117
00:06:57,582 --> 00:07:02,918
If you ever saw static on an old analog TV, about one percent of that noise was this glow.

118
00:07:02,918 --> 00:07:04,755
The afterglow of the beginning.

119
00:07:05,455 --> 00:07:06,799
Suspect four, guilty.

120
00:07:06,799 --> 00:07:09,231
So in a strange way, Olbers was right.

121
00:07:09,231 --> 00:07:12,239
Every line of sight does end on a glowing wall.

122
00:07:12,239 --> 00:07:20,495
We just can't see it, because our eyes catch only a tiny slice of light, and the universe has stretched that glow far outside it.

123
00:07:21,195 --> 00:07:22,822
And the darkness is growing.

124
00:07:22,822 --> 00:07:29,272
Expansion is speeding up, so distant galaxies are slowly being carried beyond the edge of what we can ever see.

125
00:07:29,272 --> 00:07:42,287
Physicists Lawrence Krauss and Robert Scherrer worked out that in more than a hundred billion years, astronomers in our galaxy might see no other galaxies at all, and the microwave glow would be stretched too thin to detect.

126
00:07:42,287 --> 00:07:47,109
Their night sky would be black, with no clues left that there was ever a beginning.

127
00:07:47,109 --> 00:07:47,865
We are lucky.

128
00:07:47,865 --> 00:07:49,840
We still get to read the evidence.

129
00:07:50,540 --> 00:07:51,835
So why is space black?

130
00:07:51,835 --> 00:07:56,369
Because the universe has an age, and light from most of it hasn't reached us.

131
00:07:56,369 --> 00:07:57,723
Because stars burn out.

132
00:07:57,723 --> 00:08:02,021
And because the oldest light has been stretched into colors we can't see.

133
00:08:02,021 --> 00:08:07,496
A dark night sky is one of the simplest pieces of evidence that the universe had a beginning.

134
00:08:07,496 --> 00:08:10,145
You can check it tonight, just by looking up.

135
00:08:10,846 --> 00:08:12,384
Next time, we build a star.

136
00:08:12,384 --> 00:08:13,923
What if Jupiter became one?

137
00:08:13,923 --> 00:08:17,799
How bright would our nights get, and would Earth survive the change?

138
00:08:17,799 --> 00:08:18,483
That's next.
